  "prediction": "**Summary of the Holman Rule Report**\n\nThe Holman rule is a congressional rule that separates policy and funding decisions in the House of Representatives. Established in 1835, the rule prohibits the inclusion of legislative provisions in appropriation bills unless they meet specific criteria. The rule is designed to prevent the inclusion of non-essential legislation in appropriation bills, which can lead to delays and inefficiencies in the legislative process.\n\nThe rule, also known as the \"Holman rule,\" has undergone several changes since its inception. In 1876, the rule was expanded to allow for the inclusion of provisions that reduce expenditures, such as reducing the number of federal employees or officers. However, the rule has been narrowed over time, and in 1983, it was restructured to only allow for provisions that reduce expenditures by reducing the amount of money in the bill.\n\nIn 2017, the House adopted a separate order that reinstated the older version of the rule, which allowed for provisions that reduce expenditures by reducing the number of federal employees or officers. This change opened the door to the consideration of retrenchment amendments that reduce expenditures by reducing the number of federal employees or officers.\n\nThe report highlights several precedents that have been established under the Holman rule, including the requirement that retrenchment provisions must be directly related to the reduction of expenditures and that they must not be too broad. The report also notes that the House Parliamentarian is the sole definitive authority on questions relating to the chamber's precedents and procedures.\n\nThe report concludes that the Holman rule is not intended to open the door for legislative provisions that would expand the scope of the bill, but rather to provide a limited exception to the general prohibition against legislation in appropriation bills. The report also notes that the rule is subject to interpretation and that its application may vary depending on the specific circumstances.\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n\n* The Holman rule separates policy and funding decisions in the House of Representatives.\n* The rule prohibits the inclusion of legislative provisions in appropriation bills unless they meet specific criteria.\n* The rule has undergone several changes since its inception and has been narrowed over time.\n* The 2017 separate order reinstated the older version of the rule, which allowed for provisions that reduce expenditures by reducing the number of federal employees or officers.\n* The rule is subject to interpretation and its application may vary depending on the specific circumstances.",
